1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,164
Tempo Zones are private blockchains on Tempo.

2
00:00:03,214 --> 00:00:08,215
The operator sees everything; each user sees only their own account.

3
00:00:08,265 --> 00:00:14,185
So no one outside can check that the operator ran the ledger correctly.

4
00:00:14,235 --> 00:00:17,858
Tempo's design checks batches with a hardware attestation;

5
00:00:17,908 --> 00:00:22,450
on testnet today, a placeholder verifier returns true for anything.

6
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:24,279
Sworn makes that checkable.

7
00:00:24,329 --> 00:00:27,021
For a batch the operator supplies,

8
00:00:27,071 --> 00:00:32,507
it produces a zero-knowledge proof that Tempo's own Zone code accepts it.

9
00:00:32,557 --> 00:00:38,450
Anyone can verify the proof on chain, and it exposes hashes, not transactions.

10
00:00:38,500 --> 00:00:42,669
On testnet, it worked: Tempo's own Zone verification code,

11
00:00:42,719 --> 00:00:48,763
on a test batch that contains a withdrawal, verified by a contract there.

12
00:00:48,813 --> 00:00:50,638
Here is the transaction.

13
00:00:50,688 --> 00:00:53,450
Change one field, and it's rejected.

14
00:00:53,500 --> 00:00:59,832
The first customers we're after are businesses that run Zones and answer to auditors:

15
00:00:59,882 --> 00:01:06,671
independent evidence they can match to each batch they settle — evidence, not yet a guarantee.

16
00:01:06,721 --> 00:01:09,862
Later, if Tempo builds proofs into settlement,

17
00:01:09,912 --> 00:01:14,876
withdrawals can wait for them; that design is written, not built.

18
00:01:14,926 --> 00:01:21,715
Either way, the provers must run on time and be rebuilt at each Tempo upgrade:

19
00:01:21,765 --> 00:01:24,450
the service we want to validate.

20
00:01:24,500 --> 00:01:30,850
Tempo is built on Reth and Revm, the stack I work in and teach,

21
00:01:30,900 --> 00:01:36,336
and my last project won a Uniswap Foundation prize at ETHGlobal Tokyo.

22
00:01:36,386 --> 00:01:40,450
Fifteen years in banking taught me why auditability matters.

23
00:01:40,500 --> 00:01:45,516
It's testnet and unaudited, the batches come from Tempo's integration tests,

24
00:01:45,566 --> 00:01:50,582
Moderato has one Zone operator today, and we have no customers.

25
00:01:50,632 --> 00:01:56,108
Next: one design partner, and a proof of a batch they supply.

26
00:01:56,158 --> 00:01:57,950
Sworn: Tempo's execution, proven.
